## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

## 1. Starting endpoint and attempted implication

The canonical sufficient Target B closure is

\[
G+E\ge \frac{\sqrt3}{4}B+\frac{\sqrt3}{2}(t+\chi-1).
\]

In the pure rhombic disk this reads `G+E >= (sqrt(3)/4)B`, whereas a
global minimizer satisfies the strict reverse inequality. The attempted
interface route was

```text
small physical phase cut + small original pocket roughness
  -> one dominant retained grain (PR #564)
  -> globalize a coherent-grain inequality
  -> Target B shell closure.
```

PR #564 proposes the first conditional geometric arrow, but its additional input
`Q_U=o(D)` is impossible in the canonical pure-disk survivor regime. This
report gives a direct finite obstruction and a stronger asymptotic one.

## 2. New result: atomic trains force interior pocket boundary

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting independent audit; noncanonical.

Let `U` be the union of faces of a pure, simply connected, edge-to-edge strict
unit-rhombus disk, and let `K=conv(U)`. Its boundary has `B=2T` unit-edge
occurrences, one pair of terminal edges for each of the `T` trains. Set

\[
Q_U=\mathcal H^1(\partial U\cap\operatorname{int}K),\qquad
D=\operatorname{diam}K.
\]

For any sequence of sufficiently large triangle-free pure-rhombic-disk global
minimizers in the canonical jammed branch, with `D -> infinity`,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}\frac{Q_U}{D}
\ge 4\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2\sqrt3}}
=3.8092512274\ldots .
}
\tag{2.1}
\]

In particular, the small-roughness hypothesis `Q_U=o(D)` in PR #564 has no
canonical pure-disk global-minimizer sequence to which it can apply. Section 3
replaces total roughness by boundary at fixed positive depth inside the hull.

### Finite obstruction

Write `H_U=H^1(\partial U\cap\partial K)`. Apart from finitely many
vertices, the polygonal boundary of `U` is partitioned between `int(K)` and
`\partial K`. Thus `B=Q_U+H_U`, and `H_U<=per(K)<=pi D`. Hence, without any
phase argument,

\[
\boxed{Q_U\ge B-\operatorname{per}(K)\ge 2T-\pi D.}
\tag{2.2}
\]

Canonical Section 39.7 gives
`liminf T/D >= 8/(3 sqrt(c)) = 2.5395008183...`, where
`c=2 sqrt(3)/pi`. Equation (2.2) already yields

\[
\liminf Q_U/D\ge 16/(3\sqrt c)-\pi
=1.9374089830\ldots>0.
\tag{2.3}
\]

### Proof of the stronger bound

Suppose the stated sequence exists. Section 39.1 gives `T=Theta(D)` and
`R/D^2 -> 1/c`. Pass to an arbitrary subsequence on which `T/D -> x` and
the projective-direction probability measures of the trains converge weakly
to `mu`. Let `s` be the total atomic mass of `mu`. Canonical Section 39.2
gives

\[
1/c=\lim R/D^2\le x^2s^2/4,
\qquad\text{hence}\qquad 2xs\ge 4/\sqrt c.
\tag{2.4}
\]

Fix `epsilon>0` and choose finitely many atoms of `mu` with total mass at
least `s-epsilon`. Choose disjoint projective angular neighborhoods of radius
`delta` around those atoms, with boundary carrying zero `mu` mass. Weak
convergence then shows that at least `(s-epsilon-o(1))T` trains have directions
in these neighborhoods. Their two terminal unit edges contribute at least
`2(s-epsilon-o(1))T` boundary-edge occurrences.

Only a fraction tending to zero with `delta` of those occurrences can lie on
`\partial K`. To see this without assuming that boundary edges are isolated
hull sides, scale `K` by `1/D`. The canonical area/isodiametric estimates imply
Hausdorff convergence, after translation, to the disk of diameter one.
The surface-area measures of these convex polygons therefore converge weakly
to the uniform, atom-free surface-area measure of that disk, as proved in
Section 39.2. A unit edge of `\partial U` lying on `\partial K` contributes
one unit of hull perimeter measure at a normal obtained by rotating its train
direction by a quarter turn. The chosen finitely many projective
neighborhoods correspond to finitely many pairs of normal arcs. Their limiting
surface-area measure tends to zero with `delta`. Hence the number of such
hull-terminal edges, divided by `D`, tends to zero as `delta -> 0`.

Every remaining terminal edge has its relative interior in `int(K)`, and
distinct trains have distinct terminal-edge occurrences. They are counted
by `Q_U`. First let the sequence index tend to infinity, then `delta -> 0`,
and finally `epsilon -> 0`. This gives

\[
\liminf Q_U/D\ge 2xs\ge4/\sqrt c.
\]

Because the starting subsequence was arbitrary, (2.1) holds for the whole
sequence. No source point was deleted or identified with a scaffold.

