This is a necessary geometric condition for a hypothetical sequence of
global minimizers in the pure jammed disk branch. It makes no claim about
exceptional faces, holes, multiple rhombic components, flexible survivors,
or all Level I minimizers.

## 3. Deep-boundary concentration theorem

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting independent audit; noncanonical.

Retain the canonical sequence and choose centers `z_D` such that the rescaled
convex hulls `(K-z_D)/D` converge to the radius-`1/2` disk. For fixed
`rho in (0,1/2)`, put

\[
V_{\rho,D}=B\bigl(z_D,(1/2-\rho)D\bigr),\qquad
Q_\rho(D)=\mathcal H^1(\partial U\cap V_{\rho,D}).
\]

For every fixed `rho`, `V_{rho,D}` lies inside `K` for all sufficiently large
`D`. Let `F_D` be any collection of physical rhombi. Partition the retained
faces into **edge grains**: two faces belong to the same grain if a chain of
retained faces joins them across full shared edges. Let `C_{j,D}` denote
their face unions. Vertex-only contact does not join grains. If

\[
|F_D|=o(D),\qquad Q_\rho(D)=o(D)
\quad\text{for every fixed }\rho>0,
\tag{3.1}
\]

then one retained edge grain has

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{area}(C_{*,D})=\operatorname{area}(K)-o(D^2),
\qquad R(C_{*,D})=R-o(D^2).}
\tag{3.2}
\]

All rhombi in this grain lie in one component of the retained train crossing
graph: faces sharing a full edge share the train passing through that edge.
Thus that train component also contains `R-o(D^2)` rhombi. Mere vertex
contact would not support this transfer; concurrent PR #575 gives an exact
`2 x 2` grid regression.

The second conclusion uses the fact that each rhombus has area at least
`a_0`; it applies in particular to the PC cut
when `E=o(D)`, since PC then gives `|F_D|=o(D)`.

### Proof

Fix `rho>0` and abbreviate `V=V_{rho,D}`. The canonical shell window gives
`G=area(K)-area(U)=O(D)`, so `area(V\U)=O(D)`. The total area of deleted
rhombi is at most `|F_D|=o(D)` because every unit rhombus has area at most
one. Hence the retained edge grains cover `area(V)-o(D^2)` of `V`.

For any union `S` of retained edge grains, set `A=area(S\cap V)` and let
`I=H^1(\partial(S\cap V)\cap int V)`. Two retained faces sharing a full edge
belong to the same grain. Therefore every internal boundary edge of `S`
inside `V` is either an original boundary edge of `U`, or is incident to a
deleted rhombus. Different deleted rhombi together have at most `4|F_D|`
unit sides. Vertex-only contacts have zero perimeter measure. Therefore

\[
I\le Q_\rho(D)+4|F_D|=o(D).
\tag{3.3}
\]

The finite-perimeter identity for the partition of the disk `V` gives
`per(S\cap V)+per(V\setminus S)=per(V)+2I`. Applying planar isoperimetry to
both parts yields

\[
\sqrt{\pi A}+\sqrt{\pi(|V|-A)}
\le \frac{\operatorname{per}(V)}2+I
=\sqrt{\pi|V|}+o(D).
\tag{3.4}
\]

Since `|V|=Theta(D^2)`, (3.4) says that the area fraction of **every** union
of retained edge grains in `V` tends to zero or one. If no single grain
occupied `|V|-o(D^2)`, take a largest grain when its area is a fixed
fraction of `|V|`, or greedily add smaller grains until their union first
passes a fixed fraction. Because the grains cover `|V|-o(D^2)`, this
produces a union with area fraction bounded away from both zero and one,
contradicting (3.4). Thus one edge grain `C_{rho,D}` has
`area(C_{rho,D}\cap V)=|V|-o(D^2)`.

For each fixed `rho`, the largest edge grain by total area is at least as
large as `C_{rho,D}`. Consequently

\[
\liminf_{D\to\infty}
\frac{\max_j\operatorname{area}(C_{j,D})}{|K|}
\ge (1-2\rho)^2.
\]

The hypothesis (3.1) holds for every fixed `rho`, so let `rho` decrease to
zero. This proves the area assertion in (3.2). Since the area outside the
largest edge grain is `o(D^2)` and every original rhombus has area at least
`a_0`, the rhombus population outside it is `o(D^2)` as well. This proves
(3.2). Notice that the dominant grain may depend on `D`, but the
largest-grain argument avoids any incompatibility between different
values of `rho`.

Unlike `Q_U=o(D)`, the hypothesis `Q_rho=o(D)` is not contradicted by (2.1):
atomic train ends can lie in shallow outer pockets. This theorem isolates
macroscopic deep fjords as the remaining topological obstruction. It does
not prove that canonical global minimizers lack those fjords.

The same argument gives a quantitative contrapositive. Fix
`eta in (0,1/2]`, put `rho=eta/16`, and suppose the largest retained
edge grain has area at most `(1-eta)|K|`. Since
`|V_{rho,D}|/|K|=(1-eta/8)^2+o(1)>=1-eta/4+o(1)`, for large `D` no grain
occupies more than `(1-eta/2)|V_{rho,D}|` inside the inner disk. The retained
grains cover `|V_{rho,D}|-o(D^2)`. Taking a smaller side or greedily
grouping grains gives a union whose inner-disk area fraction lies in
`[eta/8,1/2]`, up to a vanishing endpoint error. Applying (3.4) before
using (3.1) yields

\[
\boxed{
Q_{\eta/16}(D)+4|F_D|
\ge \pi\left(\frac12-\frac\eta{16}\right)
\left(\sqrt{\frac\eta8}+\sqrt{1-\frac\eta8}-1\right)D-o(D).
}
\tag{3.5}
\]

Thus two macroscopic phase-cut edge grains require **deep** pocket-boundary
length of order `D` when the physical cut has sublinear size. The unavoidable
total roughness from (2.1) is irrelevant to this test.

## 4. Frontier effect and exact remaining lemma

The canonical Target B inequality is unchanged. The conditional concentration
argument of PR #564 cannot be activated by proving `Q_U=o(D)` for this branch:
the stronger opposite bound (2.1) is necessary. Section 3 gives a replacement
which ignores shallow outer zigzags and counts only deep pocket boundary.

One precise next subproblem is:

> **Small-surplus deep-fjord exclusion.** For every fixed `rho>0`, prove that
> a sequence of triangle-free pure-rhombic-disk global minimizers satisfying
> `E=o(D)` also satisfies `Q_rho(D)=o(D)`, or construct from a contrary
> sequence an actual source-preserving unit-separated competitor of diameter
> `<D`.

This statement uses the actual source and allows `Q_U=Theta(D)`, as (2.1)
requires. Together with PC and Section 3 it would remove the
small-angular-surplus multi-grain escape; one would still need a
dominant-grain phase-density/diameter bridge and the topology/flexible cases
for Level I closure.

### Why scalar shell bookkeeping alone stalls

There is an exact currency-neutral slit operation on an **abstract selected
rhombic subcomplex**. Start with a pure unit-rhombus disk and delete `m`
faces forming a dual-tree chain, attached to the old outer boundary along
exactly one edge, while leaving the convex hull unchanged. Assume the chain
has no other boundary incidence. Its `m-1` internal shared edges give
`4m=2(m-1)+1+b_in`, so `b_in=2m+1`. The new boundary count is
`B'=B-1+b_in=B+2m`. For removed face areas `s_i`, the pocket area increases
by `sum s_i` and the angular surplus decreases by `sum(s_i-a_0)`.
Consequently

\[
(G'+E')-(G+E)=a_0m=c_0(B'-B),
\qquad
(G'+E'-c_0B')=(G+E-c_0B).
\tag{4.1}
\]

This calculation shows that the scalar shell balance cannot charge a
one-face-wide slit merely by its length. It is **not** a source construction:
deleting selected faces while keeping their vertices fixed can leave unit
contacts across the supposed slit, so the resulting subcomplex need not be
the actual contact complex of any new configuration. A proof of the
deep-fjord exclusion must use source geometry, stress, or an actual
source-preserving deformation, not only (4.1).

## 5. Regression audit

| Proposed inference | Result | Exact reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `E=o(D)` implies `Q_U=o(D)` | Rejected in the canonical pure-disk survivor regime | (2.1) forces `Q_U=Omega(D)` independently of `E` beyond canonical bounds. |
| `G=O(D)` and `|F|=o(D)` suffice for PR #564 concentration | Unsupported | Its relative perimeter identity retains the original outer boundary `Q_U`, which is necessarily linear. |
| Euclidean retained-face connectivity transfers to a train component | Rejected for vertex-only contact | Two diagonal faces in a `2 x 2` rhombus grid can meet at one vertex while their train crossing edges are disjoint; Section 3 uses edge grains. |
| A one-face-wide slit must create positive shell surplus per unit length | Rejected for selected subcomplex bookkeeping | The exact identity (4.1) makes its scalar shell increment zero; realization as a new source is not asserted. |
| PR #564 finite inequality itself is false | Not claimed | The result here addresses its application hypothesis, not the finite isoperimetric proof. |
| Phase-cut deletion removes source points | Rejected | A deleted crossing/rhombus is only an analytic decomposition; all source points remain. |

No external theorem or computation was used. The finite inequality (2.2) and
atomic proof are exact conditional deductions from the named canonical
inputs. The Section 3 proof also uses the elementary finite-perimeter
partition identity and planar isoperimetry, with the same proof structure as
the unpromoted PR #564 but on a fixed inner disk. Concurrent unpromoted PR
#575 identified the vertex-contact regression and proves a sharper finite
edge-grain interface theorem; this report uses only the elementary
`4|F|` interface count proved above. No theorem-state or
route-map file is changed pending independent audit.

## 6. Promotion recommendation and handoff

**Unreviewed candidate.** An independent audit should verify the terminal-edge
count, the surface-area-measure transfer, and the inner-disk interface count,
then record the no-go and replacement concentration consequences near the
Target B multi-grain route. The next agent should attack the small-surplus
deep-fjord exclusion above using source optimality, exact Oler payment, or a
source-preserving competitor. A relative-isoperimetric argument whose only
correction is total `Q_U` cannot close this branch.
